# .ai — rules for AI coding agents

This folder holds the architecture and naming rules that both humans and AI
coding agents follow in this workspace. The files are generated by CleanStack
and refreshed by `cleanstack regenerate`; your additions belong inside the
`<cleanstack:user-code>` region of each file so they survive regeneration.

## Rule files

| File | Contents |
| --- | --- |
| [rules/architecture.md](rules/architecture.md) | Layers, package structure, allowed dependencies |
| [rules/naming-conventions.md](rules/naming-conventions.md) | The naming contract for every artifact |
| [rules/protected-regions.md](rules/protected-regions.md) | How to edit generated files safely |
| [rules/adding-an-entity.md](rules/adding-an-entity.md) | Workflow for adding entities and features |

Entry points: [AGENTS.md](../AGENTS.md) for agents,
[ARCHITECTURE.md](../ARCHITECTURE.md) for the full-stack overview.

## How agents should use this folder

1. Load every rule file before generating or modifying code.
2. Treat the rules as constraints, not suggestions: follow them exactly so the
   generated code stays uniform.
3. When a rule blocks a change, extend the rules inside the file's protected
   region instead of breaking the rule silently.
4. Prefer generating through the CLI (`cleanstack entity <Name>`,
   `cleanstack regenerate`) over writing generated-style code by hand.

## How teams should use this folder

- Commit the folder so every agent and teammate sees the same rules.
- Add project-specific conventions inside the protected region of each rule
  file; they are merged back on regeneration.
- Change the built-in wording for all future projects with a template override:
  `cleanstack templates init`, then edit
  `.cleanstack/templates/project/root/.ai/...`.

